Ok so I made a few changes to my BSPD circuit.

After the 500 millisecond delay, I rerouted the wire to a monostable 555 timer circuit which will serve as the reactivation mechanism for our BSPD circuit. The relay is presumably going to keep the battery circuit closed when the relay is closed, and open the battery circuit when the relay powered.

Ok so the Monostable 555 timer circuit activates on a low signal sent to the TRIGGER pin. When that happens, the output will stay at HIGH for a specific amount of time which depends on the equation (T = 1.1 x R x C). If we use a capacitor with 100 microfarads and 91k ohm resistor, the output will be high for about 10 seconds. During that time, the output will power the base of a transistor, which opens the floodgate and lets current flow from power to ground for the relay, which will open the battery circuit. Essentially when the AND gate is not being fulfilled and there is no current, the 555 timer circuit will activate. When the 10 seconds are up, the transistor closes and the relay closes up the battery circuit, meaning that it is safe by the BSPD circuit's standards. Whenever the AND gate is fulfilled and there is power, the 555 timer circuit's output stays low and the relay keeps the battery circuit closed. There is a 3k ohm resistor infront of the transistor just to ensure the transistor doesn't recieve too much current, and there is a 0.01 microfarad capacitor to make the 555 timer work.
